# Why screening can look better than it is
subtitle: "How screening can add years of 'survival' without adding a single day of life, and why only fewer deaths prove it works."
voices: {"a":"gemini:Despina | a smooth, careful doctor explaining a trap; calm and clear","take":"lines"}

## Why screening can look better than it is
use: screening_timeline
with:
    onset: 1
    detectable: 2.5
    symptoms: 5.5
    death: 8.5
    screen_at: 3
    box:
      x: 50
      'y': 165
      w: 900
      h: 490
sources:
    - id: marcus2000
      title: 'Lung cancer mortality in the Mayo Lung Project: impact of extended follow-up'
      authors: Marcus PM, Bergstralh EJ, Fagerstrom RM, et al.
      year: 2000
      finding: Screening with chest X-ray and sputum cytology improved lung-cancer survival but did not reduce lung-cancer mortality (J Natl Cancer Inst 92:1308–16).
      url: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10944552/

    text yr0 "0" #5a534c x 115 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr1 "1" #5a534c x 199 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr2 "2" #5a534c x 283 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr3 "3" #5a534c x 367 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr4 "4" #5a534c x 451 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr5 "5" #5a534c x 534 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr6 "6" #5a534c x 618 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr7 "7" #5a534c x 702 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr8 "8" #5a534c x 786 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true
    text yr9 "9" #5a534c x 870 y 250 font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "Why screening can look better than it is"

Why can screening look better than it is? Survival is counted from the day of diagnosis, and screening moves that day. One patient's timeline shows how.
    draw axis yr0 yr1 yr2 yr3 yr4 yr5 yr6 yr7 yr8 yr9

First, life without screening. A cancer starts at year one, silently.
    draw row_label_0 row_0 onset_0 label_onset

By year two and a half a test could find it, but nobody is testing.
    draw detectable_0 label_detectable

Symptoms appear at year five and a half, and that is when it is diagnosed.
    draw dx_0 label_dx_0

The patient dies at year eight and a half. Survival, counted from diagnosis: three years.
    draw death_0 label_death survival_0 label_survival_0

    pause 0.4

Now the same patient and the same cancer, but screened.
    draw row_label_1 row_1 onset_1 detectable_1

A test at year three finds it, two and a half years before any symptom.
    draw dx_1 label_dx_1

Suppose treating it early changes nothing. Death still comes at eight and a half, and the dashed line shows the very same moment.
    draw death_1 sympt_guide death_guide

Now survival is five and a half years. This patient counts as a five-year survivor, and died on the same day.
    draw survival_1 label_survival_1

The gain is only lead time: the stopwatch started earlier, and the finish line never moved.
    draw lead_time label_lead

So the honest test is deaths, not survival. In a trial, do fewer screened people die of the cancer than unscreened people?
    highlight death_guide

Real trials show it. The Mayo Lung Project, begun in the 1970s, screened smokers with chest X-rays. Lung-cancer survival rose, yet lung-cancer deaths did not fall.
    text mayo "Mayo trial: survival ↑   deaths =" #b5482e cites ["marcus2000"] x 500 y 655 font_size 28

Screening can save lives, but only when finding it early moves this cross. Only a count of deaths can show that it did.
    point at.ref death_1 gesture circle

    quiz "A new screening test raises five-year survival from 40% to 70%. What does that prove?"
        right: Nothing yet: an earlier diagnosis lengthens survival even when death doesn't move. Only fewer deaths in a trial shows that screening saves lives.
        * It saves lives
        + Nothing yet: diagnosis may simply come earlier
        * Early treatment works better
---
